WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.626 --> 00:00:06.848
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.848 --> 00:00:10.670
To sum up,
we've had an amazing ride here at Tesla.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.820
And I'm so excited to lead
this company into the future.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.920
With that,
I'll take any questions from the audience.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.707
Yes.
&gt;&gt; Mr.

00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:24.994
Musk, I am a very tiny shareholder
with only [INAUDIBLE] two shares.

00:00:24.994 --> 00:00:30.415
But as all the other investors in this
room, I'm mainly interested in one thing.

00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:32.806
You guys have not made a single penny yet.

00:00:32.806 --> 00:00:35.975
When is this company
gonna become profitable?

00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:41.154
&gt;&gt; Uhh.

00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:41.887
&gt;&gt; Stop.

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:45.842
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You all know what this feels like,

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:47.260
don't you?

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.160
And this is not where you want to be.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.309
Today we will provide you with one
of your last lessons at the GSB,

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:57.640
a lesson on how to answer
tough business questions.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.470
There are three types of
tough business questions.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.220
The first are those questions for
which you do not have an answer.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.660
The second are those questions for
which you cannot provide an answer.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.010
And the third are those questions for
which you are guilty as charged.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.599
We will provide you with an example
of each one of these questions and

00:01:22.599 --> 00:01:27.310
then offer some suggestions on how to
best address each of those questions.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.504
But first, I would like to offer
credit to Jerry Weissman's book,

00:01:32.504 --> 00:01:38.257
In the Line of Fire, from which we
synthesized many of these suggestions.

00:01:38.257 --> 00:01:42.990
So what does it look like when you
don't know the answer to a question?

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.650
When is your Hawaii office opening?

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.460
I'm looking for a job.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.650
&gt;&gt; Are you kidding me?

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.920
I'm the CEO of Tesla, for crying out loud.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.880
I have better things to worry about.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.850
Maybe August or December.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.840
&gt;&gt; Stop.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:03.990
&gt;&gt; There is a much

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.740
better way to address a question for
which you do not have an answer.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.810
First, begin by rephrasing or restating
the question as you best understood it.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.630
This will do two things.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:21.650
It'll ensure that the audience member
feels heard, but it will also confirm for

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.060
you the question for which you
must ultimately provide an answer.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.700
Second, no audience member expects
you to be a walking encyclopedia.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.820
So if you don't know the answer, admit it.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.740
You really have nothing to gain and
have everything to lose by lying.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.540
If you lie,
you provide inaccurate information and

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.050
you risk damaging your reputation.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:56.000
Finally, best practice is to actually
commit to following up, and do follow up.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.533
You can even demonstrate
this intent by asking for

00:02:58.533 --> 00:03:01.860
that audience member's business card or
contact information.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.060
So let's try that again.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:08.627
When do you open your Hawaii office?

00:03:08.627 --> 00:03:11.268
I'm looking for a job.

00:03:11.268 --> 00:03:15.420
&gt;&gt; So you want to know when you might
be able to apply for a job in Hawaii?

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.610
To be honest, I don't know exactly when
our Hawaii office will be opening.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.420
However, I do know that we are rapidly
expanding all across the globe and

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.010
are looking for
talented individuals in many locations.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.100
I do actually have my head of HR,
Leslie, here with me.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.540
She'd be happy to meet with
you after this presentation.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.710
She'd know when the Hawaii
office will be opening and

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:44.660
she'd be happy to answer any other
questions that you might have.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:49.710
&gt;&gt; Now, what do you do if you receive
a question to which you cannot answer,

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.680
maybe because of confidentiality reasons?

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:53.845
Let's try this.

00:03:53.845 --> 00:03:59.300
Mr. Musk, can you comment on your
negotiations with local state

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:05.050
authorities in the jurisdictions where
Tesla cannot currently open any stores?

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.900
Have you made any political
contributions in those states maybe?

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.710
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.720
We'd be silly not to.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.970
We have a good friend, an old fraternity
brother of the CFO, Jerry Arlen.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:21.249
He's in Texas, and conveniently he sits
on the Texas Transportation Board.

00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:24.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Needless to say,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:29.050
we think we have pretty strong negotiating
leverage with the auto dealers there.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.350
We're currently in negotiations to get
full access to sell in the state of Texas,

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.820
and it should be done
in a couple of weeks.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:39.735
&gt;&gt; Wow, I'm sure Jerry Arlen will love
to see this back on television tonight.

00:04:39.735 --> 00:04:42.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; First,

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:47.530
if your company policy states that you
cannot share any information, just say so.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.190
Everyone knows that certain
information cannot be shared, and

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.309
no one will be surprised by that.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:57.379
However, try to do so in a positive way,
rather than saying our company

00:04:57.379 --> 00:05:02.702
does not share this kind of information,
try to say our company policy only

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:08.640
allows to share information that has been
previously released in press releases.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.555
Second, if you can't share certain
information, give a reason for

00:05:12.555 --> 00:05:14.860
why you can't share that information.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:22.010
You can attribute this to privacy or
security or data sensitivity, for example.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.820
Third, if you have any extra
relevant information to share, do so

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.190
with your audience.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.590
And last but not least,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:36.143
never ever answer this question by saying
if I tell you I'll have to kill you.

00:05:36.143 --> 00:05:40.473
So let's try that again.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:45.070
Mr. Musk, can you comment on your
negotiations with local state

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.880
authorities in jurisdictions where
Tesla cannot open any store currently?

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.550
Did you make any political
contributions in those states maybe?

00:05:55.010 --> 00:06:00.430
&gt;&gt; So we want customers to be able
to buy a Tesla wherever they live.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.030
And as you allude to,
unfortunately that's not today's reality.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:10.140
However, we are in negotiations with
several state authorities across

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.370
the United States, and we're working hard
to bring Tesla customers those cars.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.100
However, to keep open and
honest negotiations,

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:24.130
it's our policy not to comment
on ongoing discussions.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.887
But I can assure you that we're working
tirelessly to allow every customer to be

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.580
able to buy a Tesla where they live.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.630
&gt;&gt; That was way better.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.670
You're doing well, but
you're not of the hook yet.

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I still have one more question for

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.120
you, and word of advice for you.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.230
What should you do if
the questioner has a valid point?

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:56.300
What if we asked something that
points out one of your weaknesses?

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.019
So let's try this.

00:06:58.019 --> 00:07:03.063
Mr. Musk, how do you expect anyone
to buy a car from you if they

00:07:03.063 --> 00:07:07.820
are expected to spend 75
minutes at a charging station?

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.545
&gt;&gt; Did you get this information
from the University of Phoenix?

00:07:12.545 --> 00:07:16.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Who let this bozo in here?

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.910
No, our customers have shown
no reticence to buy cars

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.900
if they take a long time to charge.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.050
We have no problems with this at all.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:30.230
&gt;&gt; How would you feel if
you were the questioner?

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:36.630
How would you feel if a valued
concern is dismissed in this way?

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:42.719
I will explain to you a better way of
answering, a different way of answering.

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:46.780
First, paraphrase the questioner and

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:52.120
reframe the question in a way
that you show that you listened.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.840
And you change the tone of
the question to make it more positive,

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.740
to make it most promising.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:03.200
Then agree with the questioner.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.160
He has a valid point.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.710
Show him that you're on his side.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:08.610
You're not an opponent.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:15.040
And then say loud and clear but, and

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:20.470
make a U-turn to change
the tone of the answer

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:25.470
in a positive way, so
you can present evidence

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:30.629
to defend your own position while still
acknowledging the existence of this issue.

00:08:31.802 --> 00:08:38.871
And finally, you have to
conclude with a strong statement.

00:08:38.871 --> 00:08:43.170
So again, Mr. Musk,
I will give you a second chance.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.450
How do you expect people to buy a car

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.872
if they are expected to spend 75
minutes at a charging station?

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:59.960
&gt;&gt; What I'm hearing from you is that you
have a concern that people might not wanna

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.740
purchase a Tesla if it takes
a long time to recharge.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.480
I think that's a valid concern, and

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.109
it's something that I spend
a lot of time thinking about.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:16.650
Currently it takes about 40 minutes for
us to give the car an 80% car.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:19.270
And the 75 minutes you mentioned is for
a full charge.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.100
But we've been working really hard and

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:28.840
have taken several important steps
to try to mitigate this issue.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:33.910
Firstly, we just introduced battery swaps,
where people can come into a station and

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:36.160
switch in a new battery in 90 seconds.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.430
That's faster than
filling up a tank of gas.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:44.540
And secondly, we're constantly working
to improve our battery technology.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:49.530
And as we can charge the car faster, we're
gonna get people out on the road faster,

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.959
and more and more people will
wanna buy our amazing vehicles.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.262
&gt;&gt; That was the answer I
wanted to hear the first time.

00:09:57.262 --> 00:09:59.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Congratulations.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.060
So, let's recap.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.710
What did we learn today?

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:05.550
We learned how to answer three questions.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.830
When you don't know the answer,
say so and offer follow up.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.312
When you can't answer, just don't, but

00:10:16.312 --> 00:10:21.110
always provide a reasonable
explanation for not answering.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:25.722
And when the questioner has a valid point,
admit guilt.

00:10:25.722 --> 00:10:27.610
It's fine.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:28.780
It's good to connect with them.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.730
But you have to spend enough
time to defend your position and

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:40.200
to explain why the concern
has been addressed.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.690
We have seen him, how he evolved
throughout the presentation.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:50.840
He has successfully answered
three questions, and

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.874
he's ready to take
the fourth one from you.

00:10:53.874 --> 00:10:55.417
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:10:55.417 --> 00:11:01.609
[MUSIC]

